  "query_preview": "later in the week. A surface ridge stretching into the\nsoutheastern U.S. will result in light southeasterly flow, which\nwill advect in slightly lower dewpoints. However, surface\ntemperatures will likely climb a little higher due to the drier\nairmass.  Either way, it is going to be very hot. The prolonged\nnature of this heat wave compounds the risk. Remember your heat\nsafety rules and be sure to check on the elderly, children, and\npets. The strength of the upper ridge will limit the areal\ncoverage of any convection over the next few days\n\nEach day, the models are delaying the weakening of the upper\nridge. The latest model runs are less aggressive with moving the\nupper low from the Atlantic/Gulf into the Mid-South early next\nweek. Meanwhile, the models are also keeping any trough\napproaching the region from the west much further to our north. A\ncold front may push through early next week with a little rain\nand a break from the record heat, but it looks pretty weak given\nthe lack of upper support and strength of the upper ridge. The\nupper ridge will likely re-strengthen over the region as next\nweek progresses. Overall, expect mostly dry weather with above\nnormal temperatures to persist well into next week, and according\nto the medium range ensembles, probably longer than that.",
